    @section jdbc_connect_cancellation Connection establishment and timeouts

    JDBC connection factories run on a Java 21 virtual thread, allowing Qore thread cancellation and
    program interruption to interrupt Java socket connects and reads. The worker is always joined;
    connections returned concurrently with cancellation are closed. The JDBC driver class-loader context
    is preserved. Drivers that perform native I/O must honor Java interruption or provide a finite native
    login timeout; their native call cannot otherwise be forcibly stopped safely.
    @par Example
    PostgreSQL JDBC separates TCP connection and socket-read timeouts:
    @code{.py}
    Datasource ds("jdbc:app/pass@ignored{url=postgresql://db1.internal:5432,db2.internal:5432/orders?connectTimeout=5&socketTimeout=5,classpath=/usr/share/java/postgresql.jar}");
    ds.open();
    @endcode
    @note For PostgreSQL JDBC, \c connectTimeout alone does not bound a server that accepts a TCP
    connection and then stays silent. \c socketTimeout bounds socket reads, including the handshake,
    but also affects later queries. Choose both according to the application's query duration requirements.


    This module implements the \c "jdbc" driver for %Qore to allow %Qore (as well as other languages such as %Python
    that use %Qore as a "language bridge") to access data in databases with jdbc drivers.

    To use the \c jdbc DBI driver, use the driver name \c "jdbc".

    %Qore datasource strings do not follow the same format as standard Java JDBC URL strings, so some care must be
    taken when creating the %Qore datasource strings for the jdbc driver.

    The format for %Qore datasource strings with the jdbc driver is as follows:
    <tt><b>jdbc:</b>[</tt><em>user</em><tt>]/[</tt><em>pass</em><tt>]\@</tt><em>db</em><tt>[{</tt><em>option</em><tt>=</tt><em>val</em><tt>[,...]}]</tt> \n
    where all elements except <tt>\@</tt><em>db</em> are optional.

    The \a host, \a port, and \a charset values in %Qore datasource strings are ignored; the host and port must be
    given in the \a db value, and, if the jdbc driver's URL format requires the use of \c @ or \c : characters, then
    a dummy value must be provided for the \a db value, and the @ref jdbc_option_url "url option" must be used
    instead.

    Some example %Qore datasource strings for the \c jdbc driver:
    - <tt>jdbc:user/pass&#64;postgresql:dbname{classpath=/usr/share/java/postgresql.jar}</tt>
    - <tt>jdbc:user/pass&#64;firebird:?serverName=hq&databaseName=/var/lib/firebird/data/mydb.fdb&encoding=WIN1252{classpath=/usr/share/java/jna-5.12.1.jar:/usr/share/java/jaybird-5.0.0.java11.jar}</tt>
    - <tt>jdbc:user/pass&#64;ignored{url=oracle:thin:&#64;hostname:1521:dbserver,classpath=/usr/lib/oracle/21/client64/lib/ojdbc8.jar}</tt>

    @note the initial \c jdbc: in the \c db value is optional; if missing, it is added automatically by the %Qore
    \c jdbc driver when the internal URL string is passed to the internal Java JDBC call.

    @section jdbc_capabilities JDBC Driver Capabilities

    These capabilities describe what the JDBC driver can do. They are feature flags, not switches
    that enable or disable behavior. Use @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getCapabilities() "Datasource::getCapabilities()" to inspect the loaded driver;
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getCapabilityList() "Datasource::getCapabilityList()" returns readable names for the same flags.
    The connected database and client driver must also support the requested operation.
    For configurable settings and their accepted values, see @ref jdbc_driver_options.

    |!Feature|!What it means|!Capability flag
    |Transactions|Group changes into a transaction and finish it with @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::commit() "Datasource::commit()" or undo it with @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::rollback() "Datasource::rollback()".|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_TRANSACTION_MANAGEMENT "DBI_CAP_TRANSACTION_MANAGEMENT"
    |Character encoding|Convert text between Qore strings and the database connection encoding when sending and receiving values.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_CHARSET_SUPPORT "DBI_CAP_CHARSET_SUPPORT"
    |Large text and binary values|Read and bind large text and binary database values as Qore strings and binary values.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_LOB_SUPPORT "DBI_CAP_LOB_SUPPORT"
    |Stored procedures|Execute database procedures through the DBI; procedure syntax and returned values depend on the server.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_STORED_PROCEDURES "DBI_CAP_STORED_PROCEDURES"
    |Input parameters|Pass values separately from SQL with \c %v placeholders, so values do not become SQL syntax. See @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::exec() "Datasource::exec()".|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_BIND_BY_VALUE "DBI_CAP_BIND_BY_VALUE"
    |Literal SQL execution|Use @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::execRaw() "Datasource::execRaw()" to send SQL without Qore placeholder parsing. Values must already be safely represented in the SQL.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_EXECRAW "DBI_CAP_HAS_EXECRAW"
    |Prepared statements|Prepare SQL once, bind parameters, and fetch results through @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement".|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_STATEMENT "DBI_CAP_HAS_STATEMENT"
    |Single-row queries|Provide a native implementation of @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRow() "Datasource::selectRow()" for queries expected to return at most one row.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_SELECT_ROW "DBI_CAP_HAS_SELECT_ROW"
    |Arbitrary-precision numbers|Bind and retrieve decimal values using Qore arbitrary-precision numbers; database precision and configured number conversion still apply.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_NUMBER_SUPPORT "DBI_CAP_HAS_NUMBER_SUPPORT"
    |Time zone conversion|Handle time zones when converting date/time values; the database type determines whether a zone is stored.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_TIME_ZONE_SUPPORT "DBI_CAP_TIME_ZONE_SUPPORT"
    |Server time zone interpretation|Interpret date/time values using the datasource server time zone. This controls value conversion and does not change the database server configuration.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_SERVER_TIME_ZONE "DBI_CAP_SERVER_TIME_ZONE"
    |Connection recovery|Reconnect a lost connection. A lost transaction is reported to the caller; uncommitted work cannot be recovered. The interrupted statement is executed again only when it cannot have taken effect; see @ref jdbc_lost_connections.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_AUTORECONNECT "DBI_CAP_AUTORECONNECT"
    |Driver options|Read and change supported driver settings with @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getOption() "Datasource::getOption()" and @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::setOption() "Datasource::setOption()". See @ref jdbc_driver_options.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_OPTION_SUPPORT "DBI_CAP_HAS_OPTION_SUPPORT"
    |Native columnar results|Return columns suitable for analytical processing through @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectColumnar() "Datasource::selectColumnar()" and @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::fetchColumnar() "SQLStatement::fetchColumnar()" when built with columnar Qore APIs.|@ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_COLUMNAR_SELECT "DBI_CAP_HAS_COLUMNAR_SELECT"

    Native typed and columnar flags indicate optimized driver implementations. When those flags are absent,
    Qore versions providing these APIs can use their compatibility conversion paths; applications should
    check the running Qore version as well as the driver capabilities.

    See @ref jdbc_columnar_results for supported columnar types and examples.

    Statement result metadata is available through
    @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe() "SQLStatement::describe()"; see @ref jdbc_statement_describe.  This is
    separate from datasource-level @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::describe() "Datasource::describe()" and its
    @ref Qore::SQL::DBI_CAP_HAS_DESCRIBE "DBI_CAP_HAS_DESCRIBE" flag.

    @subsection jdbc_statement_describe Describing Statement Results

    @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe() "SQLStatement::describe()" returns a hash keyed by column name, as the
    rows are returned (in lower case, with a suffix for a duplicate name: \c id, \c id_1, ...), describing each
    column of the statement's result set with these keys, as other %Qore DBI drivers do:
    - \c name: the name of the column, in lower case
    - \c type: the type code of the values returned for the column (ex: \c NT_INT), given by the Java class that the
      JDBC driver reports for its values (\c ResultSetMetaData.getColumnClassName()), or by its SQL type when the
      driver does not report a class that can be loaded; values of other classes are Java objects (\c NT_OBJECT)
    - \c maxsize: the size of the column (\c ResultSetMetaData.getPrecision()): its length in characters for
      character data, in bytes for binary data, its maximum precision for numeric data; 0 if the driver reports none
    - \c native_type: the database's name of the type (\c ResultSetMetaData.getColumnTypeName())
    - \c internal_id: the \c java.sql.Types code of the column's SQL type
    - \c nullable: \c False only if the JDBC driver reports that the column cannot be null
    - \c precision and \c scale: for \c NUMERIC and \c DECIMAL columns

    For a \c NUMERIC or \c DECIMAL column with the \c optimal-numbers option (the default), \c type is \c NT_INT if
    the column has no fractional digits and at most 18 digits, otherwise \c NT_NUMBER; a value without fractional
    digits is returned as an integer in either case.

    After the last row has been read, the last result set is still described; SQL without a result set, such as DML,
    is described with an empty hash.  A statement that has not been executed yet is executed by \c describe().

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
SQLStatement stmt(ds);
stmt.prepare("select id, name, price from orders");
stmt.exec();
foreach hash<auto> col in (stmt.describe().iterator()) {
    printf("%s: %s(%d), type code %d\n", col.name, col.native_type, col.maxsize, col.type);
}
    @endcode

    @subsection jdbc_column_values Column Values

    A column value is the value that \c ResultSet.getObject() returns, converted to a %Qore value, with this
    exception:
    - the trailing spaces of a \c CHAR value are removed: a \c CHAR(20) value \c "hello" is returned as
      \c "hello" on every database, where a native %Qore driver such as \c oracle returns it padded to the size of
      the column

    @subsection jdbc_sql_errors SQL Errors

    An error that the database reports is raised as a \c JNI-ERROR exception whose argument is the
    \c java.sql.SQLException of the JDBC driver; its \c getSQLState() and \c getErrorCode() methods give the error's
    SQLState and the vendor's error code.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
try {
    ds.commit();
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    # 23503: a foreign key constraint is violated
    if (ex.err == "JNI-ERROR" && ex.arg.getSQLState() == "23503") {
        # ...
    }
    rethrow;
}
    @endcode

    @section jdbc_transaction_boundaries JDBC Transaction Boundaries

    Ordinary selects outside a transaction use JDBC auto-commit mode, so a connection returned to a
    \c DatasourcePool does not retain read locks. \c beginTransaction(), mutating datasource calls, and
    prepared statement execution start a transaction. Commit and rollback restore auto-commit mode;
    if either operation or the mode change fails, the connection is discarded instead of being reused.

    @par Explicit Transaction Example
    @code{.py}
DatasourcePool pool("jdbc:user/pass@ignored{url=postgresql://db.example.com/sales,classpath=/usr/share/java/postgresql.jar}");
pool.beginTransaction();
on_error pool.rollback();
on_success pool.commit();
hash<auto> order = pool.selectRow("select * from orders where id = %v for update", 123);
pool.exec("update orders set status = %v where id = %v", "processed", order.id);
    @endcode

    @subsection jdbc_lost_connections Lost Connections

    When a statement fails because the connection to the server was lost, the driver reconnects, and executes the
    statement once more on the new connection only if the lost attempt cannot have taken effect on the server:
    - the statement started a transaction (auto-commit is disabled, which is the default for
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::exec() "exec()", @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::execRaw() "execRaw()" and
      @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" execution), so the server rolls it back with the lost session; and
    - no earlier statement of the transaction was lost with it

    In every other case the error is raised on the reconnected datasource, and the caller decides what to do:
    - a statement executed in auto-commit mode, which is a select outside a transaction or any statement on a
      datasource in auto-commit mode, may have been completed and committed by the server before the connection
      dropped, so it is never executed twice
    - if the transaction already had executed statements, \c JDBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR is raised, as that work is
      lost; roll back the transaction before starting a new one

    Some JDBC drivers find a lost connection already when the transaction is started, before the statement is sent:
    the SQL Server driver, for example, starts a transaction with a request to the server.  Nothing can be lost or
    executed twice then, as the connection was in auto-commit mode, without uncommitted work, and no statement of the
    transaction has been sent, so the \c jdbc driver reconnects, starts the transaction on the new connection, and
    executes the statement there; if the statement then finds that connection lost as well, the rules above apply.
    If the connection cannot be re-established, the error of the lost connection is raised, followed by the error of
    the reconnection, the statement is not executed, and the next call uses a new connection.  The same applies to
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::beginTransaction() "beginTransaction()".

    SQL that starts or commits a transaction by itself, for example a batch with an explicit
    <tt>BEGIN TRANSACTION</tt> or \c COMMIT, or a procedure that commits, is outside this guarantee.
    @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" objects keep their SQL and arguments when the connection is lost and
    are prepared again on the new connection when they are next executed.

    JDBC result sets held by a \c SQLStatement remain valid across separate Java-to-%Qore calls until
    the statement is closed or its transaction ends. Each result set and statement is closed even if
    the other close operation reports an error.

    @section jdbc_multiple_results Statements with Several Results

    A statement can produce several results: a batch of statements (ex: <tt>insert ...; select ...</tt>, where the
    database and its JDBC driver accept it), or a stored procedure returning several result sets.  The driver reads
    every result, and raises the error of any statement, whether the JDBC driver reports it when the SQL is executed
    or only when that statement's result is reached (as the SQL Server driver does).  A statement with a single
    result returns it as before.

    @subsection jdbc_multiple_results_exec Results of exec(), execRaw() and select()

    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::exec() "exec()", @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::execRaw() "execRaw()" and
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::select() "select()" return a single result as before: a hash of column lists for a
    result set, or the update count (\c -1 if the JDBC driver reported no result, ex: after <tt>set nocount
    on</tt> with SQL Server).

    Several results are returned in the format of the \c freetds and \c sybase drivers:
    - result sets, and update counts greater than 0, are kept; update counts of 0 are left out, such as the counts
      that JDBC drivers report for \c SET and DDL statements, and for updates that changed no rows
    - if nothing is kept, NOTHING is returned
    - if one result is kept, it is returned alone, in the format of a single result
    - otherwise a hash is returned, with keys \c query0, \c query1, ... giving the kept results in order

    @note The shape of the value depends on the data: since an update count of 0 is left out, the same SQL can
    return a hash, a single value, or NOTHING.

    |!SQL|!Results reported by the JDBC driver|!Returned value
    |<tt>select 1 as a; select 2 as b</tt>|two result sets|<tt>{"query0": {"a": (1,)}, "query1": {"b": (2,)}}</tt>
    |<tt>update a ...; update b ...</tt>|update counts 2 and 3|<tt>{"query0": 2, "query1": 3}</tt>
    |<tt>update a ...; update b ...</tt>|update counts 0 and 3|<tt>3</tt>
    |<tt>update a ...; update b ...</tt>|update counts 0 and 0|NOTHING
    |<tt>insert ...; select count(*) as c from t</tt>|update count 1, a result set|<tt>{"query0": 1, "query1": {"c": (4,)}}</tt>
    |<tt>set search_path = app; select id from t</tt>|update count 0, a result set|<tt>{"id": (1, 2)}</tt>
    |<tt>exec proc_with_two_selects</tt> (SQL Server)|two result sets|<tt>{"query0": {...}, "query1": {...}}</tt>

    Which statements report an update count depends on the database and its JDBC driver; for example, the SQL Server
    driver does not report a count for every statement of a batch by default.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# returns {"query0": 1, "query1": {"orders": (17,)}} when the row is inserted
auto result = ds.exec("insert into orders (id) values (%v); select count(*) as orders from orders", 123);
    @endcode

    @subsection jdbc_multiple_results_select Results of selectRow(), selectRows() and selectColumnar()

    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRow() "selectRow()", @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRows() "selectRows()"
    and @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectColumnar() "selectColumnar()" return the rows of exactly one result set.
    Update counts before and after it are skipped.  SQL returning no result set raises \c JDBC-SELECT-ROW-ERROR,
    \c JDBC-SELECT-ROWS-ERROR or \c JDBC-SELECT-COLUMNAR-ERROR, as before; SQL returning more than one raises the
    same exception, for example "SQL passed to selectRows() returned more than one result set; use select() or
    exec()".

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# the update count is skipped; the rows of the select are returned
list<hash<auto>> rows = ds.selectRows("update orders set seen = true where id = %v; select * from orders "
    "where id = %v", 123, 123);
    @endcode

    @subsection jdbc_multiple_results_stmt Results of SQLStatement

    A @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" returns the rows of one result set:
    - executing it skips the update counts before the first result set; without a result set, every result is read
      when it is executed
    - when the result set has been read to the end (\c next() returns \c False, or a fetch method or
      \c getOutput() reads the last row), the remaining results are read: the errors of later statements are raised
      by that call, their update counts are added, and another result set raises \c JDBC-RESULTSET-ERROR
      ("the SQL executed by SQLStatement returned more than one result set; use select() or exec()")
    - \c affectedRows() returns the total of the update counts read so far, or \c -1 if the JDBC driver reported
      none: after executing, the counts before the result set; after \c getOutput() or \c getOutputRows(), all of
      them (%Qore allows \c affectedRows() only until rows are fetched with \c next() or a fetch method)
    - results after a result set that is not read to the end are discarded when the statement is closed or executed
      again
    - after the last row, \c fetchRow() raises \c JDBC-RESULTSET-ERROR

    @section jdbc_columnar_results jdbc Columnar Result Support

    When module-jni is built against %Qore headers exposing the columnar result API, the \c jdbc DBI driver provides
    native implementations of \c Datasource::selectColumnar() and \c SQLStatement::fetchColumnar().  Fixed-width JDBC
    result types are returned as dense %Qore buffers:
    - \c TINYINT, \c SMALLINT, \c INTEGER, and \c BIGINT map to \c buffer<*int8>, \c buffer<*int16>,
      \c buffer<*int32>, and \c buffer<*int64>
    - integer \c NUMERIC / \c DECIMAL values with precision <= 18 map to \c buffer<*int64>
    - other \c NUMERIC / \c DECIMAL values with precision <= 38 map to \c buffer<*decimal128> when module-jni
      is built against %Qore headers exposing the decimal128 buffer API
    - \c REAL, \c FLOAT, and \c DOUBLE map to \c buffer<*float32> or \c buffer<*float64>
    - \c BOOLEAN and \c BIT map to \c buffer<*bool>
    - character columns map to \c buffer<*string>
    - \c DATE and \c TIMESTAMP values carry logical date/timestamp schema metadata and map to microsecond
      \c buffer<*int64> columns when the linked %Qore runtime supports dense temporal column conversion

    Decimal values that exceed decimal128 limits, or builds against %Qore headers without the decimal128 buffer API,
    are returned as lists with JDBC precision and scale metadata.  Binary and driver-specific values are returned
    through the compatibility list path with schema metadata where the JDBC type is known.

    @par Columnar JDBC Example
    @code{.py}
Datasource ds("jdbc:user/pass@ignored{url=postgresql://db.example.com/sales,classpath=/usr/share/java/postgresql.jar}");
ds.open();

ColumnarResult result = ds.selectColumnar(
    "select id, customer_name, total, active from orders order by id");

buffer<*int64> ids = result.getColumn("id");
buffer<*string> customers = result.getColumn("customer_name");
buffer<*bool> active = result.getColumn("active");

foreach hash<auto> column in (result.schemaV2()) {
    printf("%s: %s%s\n", column.name, column.kind, column.nullable ? " nullable" : "");
}
    @endcode

    Builds against older %Qore releases that do not expose the columnar result API keep the previous \c jdbc DBI
    behavior and do not register these native columnar DBI hooks.

    @section jdbc_driver_options jdbc DBI Driver Options

    The jdbc driver supports the following DBI options:
    - \c "classpath": @ref jdbc_option_classpath "sets the classpath" with jar or class files providing the \c jdbc
      driver
    - \c "numeric-numbers": return received \c SQL_NUMERIC and \c SQL_DECIMAL values as arbitrary-precision numbers
      (Qore number values)
    - \c "optimal-numbers": return received \c SQL_NUMERIC and \c SQL_DECIMAL values as integers if possible, if not
      return them as an arbitrary-precision numbers; this is the default
    - \c "string-numbers": return received \c SQL_NUMERIC and \c SQL_DECIMAL values as strings (for backwards-
      compatibility)
    - \c "url": @ref jdbc_option_url "sets the URL" for the \c jdbc driver in case the database value in the %Qore
      datasource connection string cannot accommodate the \c jdbc URL because of special characters

    @note By default \c optimal-numbers is set on the driver for maximum compatibility with other %Qore DBI drivers

    @subsection jdbc_option_classpath jdbc classpath Option

    The \c classpath option allows for the classpath to be set before the JVM loads the Java \c jdbc driver.  The
    value of this option must be a string and is subject to environment variable substitution before being used.

    If this option is used, then the \c java.util.ServiceLoader for \c "java.sql.Driver" objects is run and iterated
    after updating the classpath, to ensure that any new drivers are loaded and available to drivers loaded after the
    initial driver is loaded, otherwise the \c java.sql.DriverManager will not see the new drivers as they were not
    present in the classpath during on-demand static initialization).

    @subsection jdbc_option_url jdbc url Option

    The \c url option allows for \c jdbc URLs to be used that contain special characters like \c @ and \c : that would
    cause the datasource to fail to parse when used as a database name.

    @par url Option Example
    @verbatim
    Datasource ds("jdbc:user/pass@ignored{url=oracle:thin:@hostname:1521:dbserver,classpath=/usr/lib/oracle/21/client64/lib/ojdbc8.jar}");
    @endverbatim

    @note the initial \c jdbc: in the url option is optional; if missing, then it is added automatically by the %Qore
    jdbc driver when the internal URL string is passed to the internal Java JDBC call.
*/
